he foreground bulb  and this is the background Bowl  and this is the original image so how do  I make these flicker well it's real  simple like I said I use the wiggle  expression so I alt or option click on  the Mac on the opacity I type in wiggle  parenthesis 10 comma 20 in parentheses  and then I hit enter on the keyboard and  for those of you who don't understand  expressions  I want the frequency to be 10 times per  second at a rate amplitude of 20 which  20 has to do with the opacity here so  since I was set originally to what was  it 69 percent capacity I'm saying you  can go up by 2 89 % or as low as 49% so  it's a factor of 20 and I'm gonna do the  same thing on the background layer  wiggle parenthesis and because this is a  little bit further in the background I'm  gonna make the frequency a little less  so let's make it 7 times per second and  20 but remember the opacity on this  layer was set to about half of what the  top layer was 32 percent so it's gonna  go up to 52 percent or all the way down  to 12 percent so instead of hitting  enter I'm just gonna click outside the  expression this time to enable it and  now when I do a preview  you can see the flickering effect taking  place and that's what I've got for you  today it's a really simple flicker glow  effect with the wiggle expression I use  this all the time on projects to make  lamps or candles or lights or or  whatever any type of light source have a  slight flicker to it it just adds a  little subtle subtlety to the to the  light effect it can be a quicker flicker  it can be a slower flicker whatever it  is for your taste but it's it works  really well and like I said I use it all  the time so that's what I got for you  this week I hope you learned something  new please like comment below until next  time I'm Cameron and this is motion  science
 

